Z||000000||BravelilFiero||12-16-2001||07:35 PM||||I just bought a 85 Pontiac fiero for about 500 bucks, said nothing was wrong with it, actually "states" they  bought 400 dollars worth of work.  Come to find out my clutch went, so I put it in the shop.  Tell the truth its still in, its been about a week and a half now....They claim, the slave was "jimmied" to stay open to make the clutch pedal to be firm...When they took off the flywheel, it had a gouge in it..When the mechanic called pontiac, they stated fiero pontiac made ****  flywheels and are no longer made????  Is this true, well any way, I bent ova and the total now is at 1100.  Well, btw, I love this site....I spent the last 3 days reading previous notes, im up to page 124, lol||||reg||1
Z||000001||GTDude||12-16-2001||08:06 PM||||I'd take the flywheel to a machine shop and have them turn it.  Very important on a hydraulic clutch system.  It must be shimmed back to the correct thickness or the clutch will not disengage.  Never done it on a fiero but done it quite a few times on s10's which has the same setup.  Good luck and welcome to the group!<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||12
Z||000002||BravelilFiero||12-16-2001||08:08 PM||||Yeah, thats what they said they did, sent it away to a machine shop, and they also sent away for a new slave cylinder.  and thankyou for the welcome||||reg||1
